  "slug": "long-zero-2365",
  "summary": "Four preprints show that explanation reliability, metric choice, and surrogate verifiers each affect which biological conclusions or experiments are supported.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is that evaluation design and surrogate choice are not neutral in biological inference. The maize Genomes-to-Fields audit formalizes prospective explanation transportability and finds that measurable reliability does not imply transport across years. The Virtual Cell Challenge 2026 preprint proposes six metrics for zero-shot CRISPRi response prediction, calibrated on experimental repeats. The closed-loop media design study reports that accuracy metrics for surrogate verifiers disagree on which experiments to run next, and that conformal calibration is examined as a remedy. BioSensor-X benchmarks evidence integration for E. coli transcription-factor prioritization with explicit coverage awareness and leakage control. The items span different domains; what is new is the emphasis on prospective, calibrated, and leakage-controlled assessment over aggregate accuracy.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
